In service of the Operations Permissions Policies project, which lets a realm grant access its coarse read/write ACL cannot express. A realm names its policy card from the `policy` field of its config card (`realm.json`). The policy card's `validate` operation tells its author which grants are in force. Two problems that take a realm's whole policy out of force are in the pointer rather than in any policy card, so a validate of the policy card can't show them:

- `policy-card-missing`: the pointer names a card the index does not hold;
- `not-a-policy`: the pointer names a card that is not a `RealmPolicy`.

Either one makes every signed-in caller the realm's ACL declines get a 500, and the only other record of why is a `realm:policy` log line, which only the operator reads. This PR shows, on the config card:

- the policy card the pointer names, rendered in its own fitted view, and opening in a new stack item when clicked;
- the pointer itself, as a small caption beneath it;
- the policy's standing: in force, or not in force with the issue that takes it out of force;
- in edit mode, the card chooser for picking, creating or removing the policy, as for a linked card.

![The config card's Policy section: the policy card's fitted view, the pointer as a caption, and a green In force badge](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cardstack/boxel/94ff4affe4ed75c13498d65102d31f9c9ee0fb8b/.pr-images/cs-13364-v4/realm-config-policy-in-force.png)

A pointer to a card that is not a policy, which renders that card and says why the policy is not in force:

![The pointer names a plain card: its fitted view, then a red Not in force badge with the not-a-policy issue](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cardstack/boxel/ee4b15cfe94f74fd0ad3b35b69dc8ab9dfa9a634/.pr-images/cs-13364-v2/realm-config-policy-not-a-policy.png)

And a pointer to a card that is not there:

![The pointer names no card: a note that there is no card at the URL, then a red Not in force badge with the policy-card-missing issue](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cardstack/boxel/ee4b15cfe94f74fd0ad3b35b69dc8ab9dfa9a634/.pr-images/cs-13364-v2/realm-config-policy-missing.png)

## The standing: an operation on the config card, not a field of realm info

`RealmConfig` declares `validatePolicy`, on the same declaration-only `validate` base the policy card uses. Invoked on the realm's own config card, `validateOperation` compiles the card the realm's pointer names. It uses the realm's own compile environment, which its policy cache also compiles with, and answers in the same `PolicyValidation` shape. A realm that names no policy answers with no `card` and empty `realms`, `issues` and `rules`. So does a realm whose pointer it couldn't read as a card's id and dropped. `PolicyValidation.card` is optional for that case alone.

It is an operation rather than a field of realm info because realm info is stamped on every card response as `meta.realmInfo`, including responses to callers a policy grant reached, and the config card is authorization infrastructure no grant reaches. An operation is gated like one. No grant reaches `validate`, and none reaches the config card, so only a caller the realm's own ACL lets read the config card gets as far as the operation.

## Who is told: the realm the pointer names is judged first

The pointer can name a card in any realm on the server, and any realm writer can repoint it. If the answer went to every reader of the config card, anyone who owns a realm could point it at a URL in a realm they can't read. `policy-card-missing` against `not-a-policy` would then tell them whether a card is there.

So before anything about the card is read, the realm holding it must be one this server serves and the caller can read (`readsRealmOf(pointer)?.read === true`). A pointer into an unreadable realm, an archived one, or one no realm here serves gets the same 403 bytes, whatever is or isn't there. An unserved realm URL is not itself a secret, but `readsRealmOf` can't tell one from a registered realm that failed to mount, whose cards are still in the index. After compiling, every other realm the compile read is judged the way a validate of the policy card judges it.

The answer is compiled fresh rather than read from the realm's policy cache, because the read check needs the compile's `reads`, which only a compile reports. The cache compiles the same card with the same environment and revalidates within five seconds of a change to anything it read, so what the card shows is in force within that bound.

## The policy card: rendered on the viewer's own session

The pointer stays a card id rather than becoming a link. A link is loaded on the realm's authority for every reader, which would hand the policy's rules to anyone who reads the config card. `PolicyCard` loads it on the viewer's session instead:

- It asks `@context.canInvoke('read', pointer)` first. It loads the card with `@context.getCard` only once the answer is `true`, then renders its fitted view inside `@context.cardComponentModifier`, so a click opens it in a new stack item as any linked card does.
- A viewer who may not read the card is told it can't be shown here, in the same words whether or not the card is there. A reader of the realm whose card is missing is told there is no card at the URL.
- Nothing is asked in a render for the indexer, so no prerendered HTML carries the card.

## The standing on the card

- **Live renders only:** `PolicyStanding` asks only in a live render, checked once when it is created. Prerendered HTML is shared by every reader of the card, and the standing is only for callers who can read the policy's realms.
- **Re-asking:** it asks again after each incremental or full index event from the config card's realm, and from every realm in the last answer's `realms`. It subscribes to those realms by difference, so an answer naming the same realms changes nothing. The realm reads the pointer from `realm.json` as saved, so a fix to the pointer clears the issue once the save lands. A fix to the policy card, or to a type its rules name, clears it when that realm indexes.
- **Stale answers are held back:** each answer is kept with the pointer the field held when it landed. An answer that names a card by URL is shown while the field resolves to that URL. Every other answer, including a refusal, is shown only while the field still holds what it did. An emptied field shows no standing.
- **What it says:** a green `Pill` reads "In force", with a count when the policy card lists issues with its grants. A red one reads "Not in force", above the explanation and the issues. An amber one reads "No policy" for a pointer the realm dropped. A realm with no policy shows nothing. Colours come from the boxel palette.

